{:edb/db-before-t 2128 :edb/db-after-t 2129 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535441 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:09:17.386Z" 13194139535441 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349320 13194139535441 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349327 13194139535441 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349337 13194139535441 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186349334 13194139535441 true] [17592186349320 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349321 13194139535441 true] [17592186349320 1089 "q-126844" 13194139535441 true] [17592186349320 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535441 true] [17592186349320 1100 "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ about the point $x=2.$" 13194139535441 true] [17592186349320 1113 #uuid "7b7eb3fc-d271-5f71-a892-30b0a27230ca" 13194139535441 true] [17592186349320 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ f(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+(\\frac{1}{2})(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-2)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535441 true] [17592186349321 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349322 13194139535441 true] [17592186349321 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349323 13194139535441 true] [17592186349321 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349324 13194139535441 true] [17592186349321 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349325 13194139535441 true] [17592186349321 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349326 13194139535441 true] [17592186349321 1040 #edb/ref 1